WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.521
Modern password storage should use established password hashing algorithms and libraries.

2
00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:16.838
Argon2, bcrypt, scrypt, and PBKDF2 are common names in this space, each with different properties, history, platform support, and tuning options.

3
00:00:16.838 --> 00:00:28.558
The right choice depends on the language, framework, regulatory environment, deployment model, and operational ability to configure and upgrade parameters safely.

4
00:00:28.558 --> 00:00:36.069
Argon2 is widely viewed as a modern choice because it can be tuned for memory cost, CPU cost, and parallelism.

5
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:43.415
Memory hardness matters because it makes large-scale guessing more expensive on specialized hardware.

6
00:00:43.415 --> 00:00:46.304
scrypt also emphasizes memory hardness.

7
00:00:46.304 --> 00:00:55.383
bcrypt has a long deployment history and is still common, though teams need to understand its limitations and parameter behavior.

8
00:00:55.383 --> 00:01:01.079
PBKDF2 remains common in standards-driven environments and some platform APIs.

9
00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:05.040
Algorithm selection should avoid custom cryptography.

10
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:18.082
Developers should not invent password hashing schemes, combine primitives casually, or depend on plain SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-512, MD5, Base64, or encryption alone for password storage.

11
00:01:18.082 --> 00:01:20.393
The hard part is not only the math.

12
00:01:20.393 --> 00:01:31.866
It is parameter encoding, salt generation, versioning, safe comparison, upgrade paths, and avoiding subtle mistakes that established libraries already handle.

13
00:01:31.866 --> 00:01:35.085
Parameter tuning is an operational decision.

14
00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:39.211
If hashing is too cheap, stolen hashes are easier to attack.

15
00:01:39.211 --> 00:01:47.713
If it is too expensive, login, registration, and reset flows may become unreliable or vulnerable to resource exhaustion.

16
00:01:47.713 --> 00:02:02.240
Teams should measure performance in their environment, document chosen parameters, revisit them periodically, and design a migration path so stored hashes can be upgraded as standards and hardware change.
